<section class="ops-intro" id="direction"><h2 data-n="03">Where we&rsquo;re at</h2>
  <p>Nathan&rsquo;s stakeholder calls on 5&ndash;6 October, followed by the discussion with Pia on 6 October.</p></section>
<div class="blk"><div class="inner">
  <p class="lead">The aim is to thank the people we work with and give them something they&rsquo;ll keep using.
    The feedback favours fewer, better items. A jacket could also reduce the number of suppliers and the
    packing work involved in the usual food hamper. Organic produce or our growers&rsquo; products are
    no longer a fixed requirement for this year&rsquo;s gift.</p>
  <div class="grid g3 rot">
    <div class="sheet"><h4>Amos Rowe</h4><p>Open to a change from hampers and likes the jacket idea.
      He wants good value for money and something different from the gift everyone else is giving.</p></div>
    <div class="sheet"><h4>Tom Rowe</h4><p>Do less and make it high quality. A jacket needs a very small,
      tasteful logo so it is something the recipient wants to wear.</p></div>
    <div class="sheet"><h4>Hamilton Rowe</h4><p>Open to jackets and a different approach to hampers.
      Any accompanying business update needs to offer something useful to the customer, with Amos as the proposed author.</p></div>
  </div>
  <p>Jamie has returned the final team allocations and supports the optional customer survey. The team feedback also puts
    useful items ahead of filler: YETI cups were well received, and Chelsea has asked that we consider
    the farm managers we deal with regularly as well as the owners.</p>
</div></div>

