  <p class="small">Source: <i>2026 Hamper Allocations - Final.xlsx</i>, Sheet1, rows 3&ndash;103; Tier 1 in column J, Tier 2 in K and notes in L. One sheet, counted once. Gift contents, complete costs and order approval remain to be agreed.</p>
</div></div>

<section class="ops-intro" id="customer-feedback"><h2 data-n="07">Jacket size &amp; customer feedback</h2>
  <p>Use the size request to invite a short, optional service and marketing survey. Jamie supports the optional survey; the draft now reflects his feedback.</p></section>
<div class="blk"><div class="inner">
  <p>The current Week 41 sales and marketing working notes raise product problems, delivery timeframes and actual results,
    along with clearer expectations and order updates. The survey should help us see which parts of the experience need attention
    and hear what is working well.</p>
  <p>Keep the size details and service questions together in one short form, with feedback clearly optional.
    The gift is a thank you; it does not depend on completing the survey or giving a favourable score.</p>
  <p><b><a href="customer-survey-2026.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Preview the draft jacket-size and customer survey &rarr;</a></b>
    &middot; <a href="./#surveys" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open Surveys in the master dashboard</a>.
    The preview lets us review the questions and flow; it does not send or store answers.</p>
  <div class="note t"><h4>Sales manager feedback &middot; 9 October</h4><p>Jamie supports the optional questions. Following his suggestion, the order-accuracy rating has been removed to keep the form focused on feedback that will be useful. The eight remaining ratings, written feedback and marketing preferences stay in place.</p></div>
  <h3 class="sub">Proposed invitation</h3>
  <div class="email-draft" aria-label="Proposed jacket size and customer feedback email">
    <p class="subject">A small thank you from Hybrid-Ag</p>
    <p>We&rsquo;re putting together something a little different for our end-of-year gifts this year and would like to send you a Hybrid-Ag jacket.</p>
    <p>Please let us know your preferred jacket size so we can get it right. We&rsquo;ll include the sizing guide for the jacket we&rsquo;re sending you.</p>
    <p>While you&rsquo;re there, we&rsquo;d also appreciate a few quick ratings on your experience with us, anything we could improve, and what information or resources you&rsquo;d find useful.</p>
    <p>The feedback is optional. We&rsquo;ll send your jacket whether or not you answer those questions.</p>
    <p>Thank you for working with us.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="survey-draft" aria-label="Draft customer survey questions">
    <h3 class="sub">Part 1 &middot; Your jacket</h3>
    <p>Name and business or farm; preferred jacket cut and size, using the selected jacket&rsquo;s own size guide.
      Include an <b>Other / help with sizing</b> option. Ask for a size when Other is selected; extra details are optional when asking for sizing help. Available sizes and cuts must match the final supplier order.</p>
    <h3 class="sub">Part 2 &middot; Your experience with Hybrid-Ag</h3>
    <p>Thinking about your experience with Hybrid-Ag this year, how would you rate each of the following? <b>1 = very poor &middot; 10 = excellent</b>.
      Choose <b>N/A / Cannot rate</b> if an area isn&rsquo;t relevant or you can&rsquo;t rate it yet. Choose N/A for problem handling if you haven&rsquo;t raised a problem.</p>
    <div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Area to rate</th><th>Response</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>How promptly we respond when you contact us</td><td class="response">1&ndash;10 / N/A</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Usefulness of our agronomy advice</td><td class="response">1&ndash;10 / N/A</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Quality of the Hybrid-Ag products you receive</td><td class="response">1&ndash;10 / N/A</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Results you&rsquo;ve seen from our products or prescription blends</td><td class="response">1&ndash;10 / N/A</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Value for money</td><td class="response">1&ndash;10 / N/A</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Deliveries arriving within the timeframe agreed with you</td><td class="response">1&ndash;10 / N/A</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Updates about your order and delivery</td><td class="response">1&ndash;10 / N/A</td></tr>
      <tr><td>How well we dealt with any problems you raised</td><td class="response">1&ndash;10 / N/A</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <h3 class="sub">Optional written feedback</h3>
    <p><b>If the amount you buy from Hybrid-Ag has changed this year, what is the main reason?</b></p>
    <p><b>What is the main thing we could do better, if anything?</b> You can also tell us what is working well, or anything else we should know.</p>
    <h3 class="sub">Part 3 &middot; Useful information from us</h3>
    <p><b>Which topics or resources would you like more of?</b> Choose up to three: seasonal crop nutrition advice;
      understanding test results; product and prescription-blend use guides; customer examples and trial results;
      ordering and delivery information; Other; or Nothing extra.</p>
    <p><b>How would you prefer us to share it?</b> Choose up to two: email updates; short videos; downloadable guides;
      webinars or Q&amp;A; printed information from your agronomist; a discussion with your agronomist; or No preference.</p>
  </div>
  <p>Review the ratings by area rather than reducing everything to one overall score. Exclude N/A answers from any averages.
    Keep the comments with the account record, pass issues to the relevant sales, technical or operations person, and track what was done and whether the issue was closed out.</p>
  <p class="small">The interactive draft is available under Surveys. Connect private response collection after approval;
    no invitations or surveys have been sent. Replies would be identifiable alongside the size request, so do not describe the survey as anonymous.
    Gift recipients are a selected group: their replies can guide service improvements, but do not establish why sales have changed across the whole business. Use them alongside Jamie&rsquo;s separate win-back conversations with lapsed clients.</p>
</div></div>

<section class="ops-intro" id="next-steps"><h2 data-n="08">What happens next</h2>
  <p>Resolve the client notes and complete costs, then agree the final gift before placing orders.</p></section>
<div class="blk"><div class="inner">
  <div class="tw"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>Owner</th><th>Current position</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Resolve the remaining allocation notes</td><td>Jamie + relevant sales people</td><td>Final team quantities received: 50 Tier 1 / 83 Tier 2. Six client notes are shown above; agree any extra quantities or custom gifts before costing them.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Complete the item and branding quotes</td><td>Pia, with Paige for embroidery</td><td>Pia&rsquo;s itemised prices and photos are now in the two proposed tiers. Reconcile GST, engraving, setup and freight costs (the Modena quote already includes its own), the quoted $7.95 jacket embroidery, quantity conditions and lead times.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Confirm the working bag choice</td><td>Pia + Nathan</td><td>Modena at $12.78 each (incl. GST, $80 setup fee and $50 freight) / 100 MOQ (to confirm) is provisionally included across both tiers. Confirm material, small logo print, packed fit, complete price basis and lead time.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Review the current packages and jacket alternatives</td><td>Nathan + Amos</td><td>The same non-hooded Kathmandu Epiq is the default for both tiers. Item spend: $32,922.24, or $29,867.84 using the Macpac Tier 2 budget alternative. These now include one Modena bag per gift. Confirm supply and review Tier 2&rsquo;s $8.78 overrun against the $200 target before remaining costs.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Agree the size request and customer survey</td><td>Nathan + sales manager; Pia coordinates</td><td>Jamie supports the optional survey; the order-accuracy question has been removed. Eight ratings, optional comments and marketing preferences remain under Surveys. Finalise wording, sizing guide, private response collection and follow-up before inviting customers.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Discuss a thank-you letter and useful business update</td><td>Nathan + Amos</td><td>Planned for the Thursday 8 October stakeholder discussion. Content and release still to agree.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Clarify gifts for day-to-day farm managers</td><td>Nathan + Jamie, with agronomists</td><td>Discuss who should receive them, whether handed over separately, and how they sit within the budget.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Agree gifts, collect sizes and order</td><td>Nathan + Amos; Pia coordinates</td><td>After quantities, costs, supply and final direction are confirmed. Schedule delivery before the end-of-year event once timing is settled.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <h3 class="sub">Ideas still open</h3>
  <p>A small gift handed directly to a farm manager could recognise the person the agronomist works with
    day to day. A fold-out thank-you note could also leave space for an optional handwritten message from
    the agronomist. Neither the extra recipient numbers nor the final note format are settled.</p>
  <p>An Amos letter could include a useful business update, if there is news ready to share.
    The proposed subjects and their relevance to clients need to be agreed with him. A new site or app
    should only be mentioned when its status and timing are clear.</p>
  <p>Testing credits and a more flexible gift budget linked to each client&rsquo;s sales and margin are ideas
    for further discussion with Jamie, with no decision made. The foliar sample pack is not a current inclusion:
    Amos questioned its value for clients already using the products, and no new product was identified.
    The cookbook is also no longer a priority for the current proposal.</p>
  <div class="src">
    <h3 class="sub">Current sources</h3>
    <p><a href="https://app.notion.com/p/Week-41-52-05-09-10-26-3ed1966d6fde81fb9dbbd100e92f5d9b" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Week 41/52 planning and stakeholder call notes</a>
      &middot; <a href="https://youtu.be/uKL3aOOtam4" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nathan and Pia&rsquo;s hamper discussion, 6 October 2026</a>.</p>
    <p>The top two proposals follow Pia&rsquo;s 8 October itemised live-page update and Nathan&rsquo;s instruction to bring those options, exact prices and supplied photos forward for Amos&rsquo;s review.
      Nathan&rsquo;s later 8 October refinement makes the Kathmandu Epiq the default jacket for both tiers, with the Macpac Halo retained as the Tier 2 budget alternative.
      Final quantities and exact client notes come from <i>2026 Hamper Allocations - Final.xlsx</i>, Sheet1, supplied by Nathan on 9 October. This supersedes the earlier draft workbook quantities.
      Jamie&rsquo;s accompanying email supports the optional survey and suggests removing order accuracy; Nathan agreed to that change on 9 October.
      The proposed survey draws on the current Week 41 <i>07/10/26 Working Draft</i>; reported issues are planning inputs, not established causes of a wider sales decline.
      Item ranges, quote details and photos were supplied by Nathan on 7 October in <i>Hampers - Quotes.docx</i> and <i>Hamper Items - Photos.zip</i>.
      Prices are recorded sourcing information and still need a complete supplier quote before ordering. Older records and supplier references remain below.</p>
  </div>
  <details><summary>Watch the 6 October discussion</summary>
    <div class="session" style="margin-top:16px">
      <div class="vid"><iframe loading="lazy" src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/uKL3aOOtam4" title="Nathan and Pia — 2026 hamper direction — 6 October 2026" allow="accelerometer; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen></iframe></div>
      <div><p class="s-kick">2026 direction &middot; 6 October</p><h4>Stakeholder feedback and gift options</h4>
        <p class="s-meta">Nathan Katseli &amp; Sophia Ong (Pia) &middot; approximately 31 minutes</p>
        <p>The recording covers the move towards quality jackets, the team&rsquo;s ideas, farm-manager gifts,
          sourcing and the information needed to settle the costs. The written update above includes the later tier proposal and Jamie&rsquo;s sent deadline.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
