</div></div>

<details class="archive" id="history">
  <summary>2025 record &amp; earlier 2026 planning<small>Historical reference. The current October direction above supersedes earlier gift inclusions, owners and sourcing plans.</small></summary>
  <div class="blk"><div class="inner"><div class="note"><h4>Earlier planning, retained for context</h4>
    <p>The material below records the 2025 cycle and the 2026 ideas developed before the October calls.
      It is not the current order list. In particular, the earlier foliar pack, cookbook, no-caps rule,
      beanie-first tier and Hamilton-led allocation process have been overtaken by the current proposal and Jamie&rsquo;s allocation request.</p>
    <p>The 2025 record shows 171 hampers: 27 large, 74 medium and 70 small, within an approximately $35,000 budget.
      Stock-on-hand notes below are from the earlier record and need a fresh count.</p></div></div></div>
<section class="ops-intro"><h2 data-n="01">Why hampers, and why they sit here</h2>
  <p>Not a generic supplier gift &mdash; a working piece of the community &amp; partners pillar.</p></section>
<div class="blk"><div class="inner">
  <p class="lead">The hamper is a <b>reciprocity tool</b>. It fills the goodwill bar with the sales team and
    with every client who receives one &mdash; which makes the next conversation, the next order and the next
    content ask easier. It is also deliberately built from our own ecosystem: the wine, macadamias, olive oil,
    almonds, dried fruit and flour are bought from growers we work with, so one budget strengthens two
    relationships at once. Coordination runs across Marketing, Sales, Accounts, Admin and Management &mdash;
    in 2025 that cross-functional alignment was markedly better than the year before.</p>
  <blockquote>&ldquo;Thanking Hybrid for a generous and thoughtful hamper &mdash; the whole family enjoyed it.&rdquo;
    <span class="who">Unprompted client email, January 2026. Taravale also featured our 1&nbsp;kg flour order in their own
    behind-the-scenes Instagram reel &mdash; their first large order.</span></blockquote>
</div></div>

<section class="ops-intro"><h2 data-n="02">The 2025 cycle &mdash; what shipped, what it taught us</h2>
  <p>Three tiers, 171 crates, ~$35K. A clear uplift in execution &mdash; and a short, honest list of what to fix.</p></section>
<div class="blk"><div class="inner">
  <h3 class="sub">What actually shipped &mdash; the three 2025 tiers</h3>
  <div class="tierlays">
    <figure><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Large hamper contents"><figcaption><div class="fc" style="color:var(--berry)">Large &middot; $400 &middot; &times;27</div><div class="fd">Kaesler Love Child &middot; both macadamia lines &middot; balsamic &middot; Yeti cup + bottle &middot; beanie &middot; notepad</div></figcaption></figure>
    <figure><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Medium hamper contents"><figcaption><div class="fc" style="color:var(--teal)">Medium &middot; $200 &middot; &times;74</div><div class="fd">Lienert GMM / Handpicked Pinot &middot; olive oil &middot; macadamias &middot; dried fruit &middot; flour &middot; beanie</div></figcaption></figure>
    <figure><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Small hamper contents"><figcaption><div class="fc" style="color:var(--orange)">Small &middot; $120 &middot; &times;70</div><div class="fd">Longview Vista Shiraz Barbera &middot; olive oil &middot; almonds &middot; macadamias &middot; flour &middot; beanie</div></figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="sheet" style="--a:var(--green)">
      <h4>What worked</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Crates + engraved acrylic lids + black palette</b> &mdash; premium finish, stackable, no cellophane fiddle. Slats double as handles.</li>
        <li><b>Client-name labels on every crate</b> &mdash; killed the mix-ups and enforced conditions (dairy farmers get no almonds; wine clients never get their own wine back).</li>
        <li><b>Production-line packing</b> &mdash; instructions on the wall, a sample to copy. Paige + Monique + Chelsea ran it in about a week.</li>
        <li><b>Premium branded items</b> &mdash; the black beanie in every crate, engraved Yeti for the top tier. Lifted the whole experience.</li>
        <li><b>Pia&rsquo;s documentation</b> &mdash; allocation list, conditions sheet, tier-based packing list, supplier context, live delivery checklist. This is why 2026 is repeatable.</li>
        <li><b>Family-friendly product mix</b> &mdash; the crate lands at the house, not the shed. Produce the whole family eats is why it gets remembered (and why the thank-you note came from the whole family).</li>
        <li><b>Supplier collaboration</b> &mdash; discounts and flexibility nearly everywhere (15% off Kaesler, 35% off Handpicked, freight covered on dried fruit, nine bonus flour bags from Col at Taravale for staff).</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="sheet" style="--a:var(--red)">
      <h4>What didn&rsquo;t</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Calendar ownership was unclear</b> &mdash; Amos pre-ordered stock calendars for VARD without flagging Marketing. 2026: Marketing owns calendars, full stop.</li>
        <li><b>Allocations changed mid-flight</b> &mdash; 2026 gets a firm cut-off date; a late case has to be genuinely strong.</li>
        <li><b>Content capture didn&rsquo;t happen</b> &mdash; deliveries ran through December and the reciprocity bar with sales was still empty. 2026: expectations set on day one, built through individual asks. The one asset that did get captured was <a href="https://photos.app.goo.gl/KoNbPUnC6EJUeksG7" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Woody&rsquo;s delivery video</a> &mdash; the whole 2025 haul.</li>
        <li><b>Card-signing session never ran</b> &mdash; ad-hoc notes instead. 2026: formal boardroom session, aligned with the team&rsquo;s in-person review days.</li>
        <li><b>Hats arrived dusty</b> &mdash; a $1 lint roller fixes it. Now a pre-pack QC step.</li>
        <li><b>Recipe book + territory map didn&rsquo;t make the cut</b> &mdash; both carry into 2026.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <h3 class="sub">The supplier network &mdash; who we buy from, and why it matters</h3>
  <div class="tw"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Supplier</th><th>Contact</th><th>Notes from 2025</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Crates (3 sizes) + acrylic engraved lids</td><td>Aussie Wine Boxes &amp; Crates, Bendigo</td><td>Maree</td><td>Husband-and-wife operation &mdash; <b>book early, lead time is the whole game</b>. She re-cut the small crate to hold budget; call her first in 2026 (and take her a box of chocolates).</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Wine (one bottle per hamper)</td><td>Kaesler &middot; Lienert &middot; Handpicked &middot; Longview</td><td>via Nate S. &amp; Lintern</td><td>SA regenerative/organic mix + client vineyards. 15&ndash;35% discounts; Longview at $19.60/bottle. Never return a client&rsquo;s own label to them. 2026 shortlist also carries <b>Hither &amp; Yon</b>, <b>Inkwell</b> and <b>Torbreck</b> off the SA regenerative/organic list, plus client vineyards.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Macadamias &mdash; roasted/salted + cold-pressed oil</td><td>Red Rock Macadamias</td><td>Jason Klotz</td><td>Wholesale pricing. Premium provenance &mdash; a client Woody has built into a serious account.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Organic whole almonds</td><td>Manna Farms (Dave&rsquo;s Nuts)</td><td>David Keens</td><td>Personally delivered the almonds. Easy to work with.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Dried fruits</td><td>Olive Grove Fruits (Little Gemz)</td><td>Sharee Gallace</td><td>Discounted + covered more than half the freight; wholesale on offer if orders continue.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Olive oil</td><td>Goldi (Oasis) &middot; Cockatoo Grove</td><td>Owen Symington</td><td>$9 off per bottle from Goldi. Cockatoo Grove is the second approved source on the inclusions list &mdash; decide which one runs in 2026.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Flour</td><td>Taravale</td><td>Col Harper</td><td>15+ years regenerative &mdash; brand story aligns. Bulky and heavy though: challenge whether it stays inside the crate in 2026.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Licorice</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>Carry-over inclusion, pairs with the Taravale flour. Supplier to confirm at the October sourcing pass.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Branded notepads</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>Replaced the Hybrid diary &mdash; cheaper, used more, no dated stock left over. Carry into 2026.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Balsamic vinegar</td><td>Random Harvest</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>Large hampers only.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Beanies (all crates)</td><td>Bullseye Gifts</td><td>Skye Dawson</td><td>Black wool, embroidered logo. Repeat supplier, gives discount &mdash; reuse the 2025 SKU.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Yeti cup + bottle (top tier)</td><td>YETI Australia</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>Black, engraved. Order early &mdash; 2025&rsquo;s landed with days to spare.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
</div></div>

<section class="ops-intro"><h2 data-n="03">The sessions &mdash; the record behind every decision</h2>
  <p>Three recorded meetings carried the program from history-gathering to a closed loop. The notes below are distilled from the full transcripts.</p></section>
<div class="blk"><div class="inner">

  <div class="session" style="--a:var(--teal)">
    <div class="vid"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/GoxNQ_Zksss" title="Hamper Planning — Katie Rowe — 8 Oct 2025" allow="accelerometer; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen></iframe></div>
    <div>
      <p class="s-kick">Session 1 &middot; Discovery</p>
      <h4>History &amp; supplier baseline &mdash; with Katie Rowe</h4>
      <p class="s-meta">8 October 2025 &middot; Nathan Katseli &amp; Katie Rowe</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Katie walked through the 2022&ndash;24 playbook: crates beat baskets and long boxes; the cellophane-and-ribbon year took far longer; the etched acrylic lid is the premium look.</li>
        <li>~25&ndash;30 hampers left over from 2024 &mdash; an allocation gap, not a budget one. Leftover chocolates had melted upstairs: no meltables, ever.</li>
        <li>Ideal rhythm: orders in within 1&ndash;2 weeks of early October, packed before 1 November, delivered mid-Nov to mid-Dec.</li>
        <li>Tiers simplified on the call from four-way confusion to $400 / $200 / $120; allocation by GP + relationship (Amos&rsquo;s steer), Hamilton to own the list.</li>
        <li>The Costco insight: when Katie shopped in July she bought whisky at half retail &mdash; <b>same budget, earlier sourcing, visibly bigger hampers</b>.</li>
        <li>Beanie for everyone (caps are an unwinnable style debate); engraved Yeti reserved for the top tier; avoid knives (freight) and $15&ndash;20 oil spray bottles.</li>
        <li>Calendars: same stock artwork for years, surplus left behind, reps forget to take them &mdash; the seed of the client-photo calendar built from Hamilton&rsquo;s farm photography.</li>
        <li>Personalisation beats value: Torrens Valley gets Bundaberg rum because that&rsquo;s what he likes. Worth a preferences tab in the CRM.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="session" style="--a:var(--orange)">
    <div class="vid"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/zUYheNXYic8" title="Hampers — Packing & Logistics — 11 Nov 2025" allow="accelerometer; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen></iframe></div>
    <div>
      <p class="s-kick">Session 2 &middot; Packing &amp; logistics</p>
      <h4>Locking the on-the-ground plan</h4>
      <p class="s-meta">11 November 2025 &middot; Sophia Ong (Pia), Nathan Katseli, Paige Cox, Katie Rowe</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Katie now Melbourne-based &mdash; remote advisory only. Paige leads packing on-site with Monique as buddy; Chelsea in for a half/full day.</li>
        <li>Crates ready Friday 21 Nov; packing complete by 28 Nov; ~170 crates is a one-week job once everything lands.</li>
        <li>The decision of the session: <b>a name label on every crate</b> &mdash; customer + agronomist &mdash; so conditions hold even when reps grab and go.</li>
        <li>Personalised cards: Amos polished the thank-you copy, back-to-back print, team writes messages around it, card travels inside the crate &mdash; signed before packing even starts.</li>
        <li>Delivery tracking moved from a printed tick-sheet to a live Odoo Knowledge checklist, shareable by link, reviewed at sales meetings.</li>
        <li>Pia&rsquo;s supplier tracker: 13 line items, tracked to the day &mdash; the visibility that made the week possible.</li>
        <li>Packing space defaults to the storeroom; confirm with Amos (warehouse works were underway) and borrow muscle from the warehouse crew for carrying.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="session" style="--a:var(--berry)">
    <div class="vid"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/-SUqx_Ih7Vg" title="Hampers 2025 — Result & Review — 27 Jan 2026" allow="accelerometer; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen></iframe></div>
    <div>
      <p class="s-kick">Session 3 &middot; Result &amp; review</p>
      <h4>Closing the loop &mdash; and setting up 2026</h4>
      <p class="s-meta">27 January 2026 &middot; Sophia Ong (Pia) &amp; Nathan Katseli</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Verdict: a clear uplift in execution quality and structure &mdash; better documentation, coordination and far less pressure than 2024.</li>
        <li><b>Two tiers for 2026</b>, locked on the call: a standard and an extra-special. Firmer with sales &mdash; lock allocations at the meeting or miss out.</li>
        <li>Allocations fall out of Hamilton&rsquo;s forecasting tiers &mdash; ask for the list months earlier, then simply confirm outliers with the team.</li>
        <li>Planning and ordering both <b>pulled forward a month &mdash; September</b>, on the logic that finished-early hampers just sit ready.</li>
        <li>Costco/Aldi sourcing meeting with Katie before lock-in; reassess Yeti&rsquo;s range at the time (the cooler-as-crate idea stays parked on cost).</li>
        <li>Foliar 3-pack confirmed as the cheap, high-novelty 2026 addition &mdash; we manufacture the product, we only buy bottles.</li>
        <li>Recipe book: refresh Chris&rsquo;s 2022 design, regenerate recipes with AI around what&rsquo;s actually in the crate.</li>
        <li>The reciprocity-bar frame (from <i>Influence</i>): fill it individually &mdash; Chelsea&rsquo;s magazine subscription &rarr; her data &rarr; her hamper-handover video.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

</div></div>

<section class="ops-intro"><h2 data-n="04">Earlier 2026 proposals</h2>
  <p>Recorded before the October direction change; retained as history, with current decisions shown above.</p></section>
<div class="blk"><div class="inner">
  <h3 class="sub">Two tiers, allocated by GP + relationship</h3>
  <div class="tw"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>~Budget</th><th>Audience</th><th>Flagship</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="tag prem">Premium</span></td><td class="n">~$400</td><td>Top clients &mdash; ~$1M+ spend or a relationship worth investing in</td><td>Engraved black Yeti cup + bottle</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="tag std">Standard</span></td><td class="n">~$200</td><td>Broader high-value and strategic accounts</td><td>Black embroidered beanie</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p style="max-width:86ch">Hamilton owns the allocation list and delivers it <b>early, out of the forecasting
    cycle</b> &mdash; not in November. Amos&rsquo;s criterion stands: gross profit plus relationship potential,
    not raw spend. One firm cut-off date; after that, only a genuinely strong case moves a name.</p>

  <div class="grid g4 rot" style="margin-top:18px">
    <div class="sheet"><h4>New &middot; Foliar 3-pack</h4><p>RF House Plants + Product Green + one TBC with Hamilton. We manufacture it, so it&rsquo;s nearly free &mdash; and it&rsquo;s the most on-brand item in the crate. Lock the third product early.</p></div>
    <div class="sheet"><h4>New &middot; Client-photo calendar</h4><p>Marketing-owned centerpiece: 12 months of Hamilton&rsquo;s real client-farm photography replacing stock art. <b>Amos does not pre-order</b> &mdash; he flags VARD timing only.</p></div>
    <div class="sheet"><h4>New &middot; Recipe book refresh</h4><p>Chris&rsquo;s 2022 design as the base; AI-regenerated recipes tied to what&rsquo;s actually in the hamper. Tangible, cheap, and the groundwork already exists.</p></div>
    <div class="sheet"><h4>New &middot; Card-signing session</h4><p>Formal boardroom session aligned with the team&rsquo;s in-person review days. Cards signed before packing starts, then sealed inside each crate.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="note r">
      <h4>Earlier hamper rules &mdash; historical</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><b>No knives</b> &mdash; freight and safety.</li>
        <li><b>No chocolate or meltables</b> &mdash; 40&deg;C delivery runs; the 2024 leftovers melted upstairs.</li>
        <li><b>Never return a grower&rsquo;s own produce</b> &mdash; substitute an equivalent.</li>
        <li><b>No individual oil spray bottles</b> &mdash; $15&ndash;20 of glass nobody asked for.</li>
        <li><b>No caps</b> &mdash; the style debate is unwinnable. Parked indefinitely.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="note t">
      <h4>Quality control &mdash; before anything ships</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>Build <b>one sample of each tier first</b> &mdash; photograph it; if it looks short, add filler or minor ribbon.</li>
        <li><b>Lint-roll / brush every branded item</b> &mdash; the 2025 hats arrived dusty.</li>
        <li><b>Pre-label every crate</b> with client + agronomist name before packing.</li>
        <li>Keep the contents palette <b>black / grey coordinated</b> &mdash; it&rsquo;s why the crates read premium.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h4>The early-sourcing play</h4>
    <p>Katie&rsquo;s July Costco runs bought whisky at half retail and premium goods at deep discount. Starting in
    September buys back that advantage: <b>the same ~$35K delivers visibly more per crate</b>. Run the sourcing
    meeting with Katie before any order is locked &mdash; and call Maree at Aussie Wine Crates first, because her
    lead time sets the whole calendar.</p>
  </div>
</div></div>

<section class="ops-intro"><h2 data-n="05">The 2026 runway</h2>
  <p>The earlier hamper-based schedule, retained for reference. The current gift format and timing still need confirmation.</p></section>
<div class="blk"><div class="inner">
  <div class="tw"><table class="tl">
    <thead><tr><th>When</th><th>What happens</th><th>Who</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr data-m="9"><td>Sep &middot; Plan + lock</td><td>Hamper Planning Session &mdash; Pia + Nathan + Katie (remote). Allocation list from Hamilton out of forecasting. Confirm Amos is <b>not</b> pre-ordering calendars. Costco/Aldi sourcing pass with Katie. Confirm two tiers + ~$35K envelope. Re-survey sales for <i>new</i> ideas only. <b>Proposal to Amos for sign-off</b> &mdash; tiers, budget, allocation criteria &mdash; before anything is ordered.</td><td>Pia leads &middot; Nathan &middot; Katie &middot; Hamilton (list) &middot; Amos (sign-off)</td></tr>
      <tr data-m="10"><td>Oct &middot; Source + order</td><td>Call Maree first &mdash; crate lead time anchors everything. Finalise foliar 3-pack with Hamilton. Dig out Chris&rsquo;s recipe-book file, regenerate recipes. Pull 12 calendar selects from Hamilton&rsquo;s photos. Reorder beanies + Yetis on 2025 SKUs. Place all supplier orders &mdash; Red Rock, Manna Farms, Olive Grove, Goldi, wine list, Taravale, licorice.</td><td>Pia &middot; Hamilton (foliar + photos) &middot; Katie (sourcing)</td></tr>
      <tr data-m="11"><td>Nov &middot; Pack</td><td>Confirm packing space with Amos (storeroom default). Pia stands up the live Odoo Knowledge checklist &mdash; conditions, red flags, delivery tracking. Boardroom card-signing session on review days. Sample build of each tier, then the production line: Paige leads, Monique buddy, Chelsea support. Labels on, lint-roll pass, pre-pack QA.</td><td>Paige leads &middot; Monique &middot; Chelsea &middot; Pia (checklist) &middot; Amos (space)</td></tr>
      <tr data-m="12"><td>Dec &middot; Deliver</td><td>Everything packed by ~28 Nov / early Dec. All hampers delivered <b>before the Hybrid-Ag EOY event</b> &mdash; that&rsquo;s the anchor. Sales tick the checklist as they go; Marketing flags content-capture asks during the delivery window.</td><td>Sales team &middot; Marketing</td></tr>
      <tr data-m="1"><td>Jan &middot; Review</td><td>Result &amp; Review session with Pia. Update the Notion record with 2026 results and the 2027 deltas &mdash; the loop that makes next year easier again.</td><td>Pia &middot; Nathan</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="note">
    <h4>Stock on hand going in</h4>
    <p>Two assembled 2025 hampers still with Amos (Fusion Fertiliser&rsquo;s and one individual client&rsquo;s), a box of leftover supplier stock and two boxes of Kaesler &mdash; filler and Amos-discretion gifts for this cycle. Count it before the October orders go in.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 class="sub">Who owns what</h3>
  <div class="tw"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Owner</th><th>2026 note</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Marketing Manager &mdash; oversight + strategic gates</td><td><b>Nathan Katseli</b></td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Assistant Marketing Manager &mdash; lead execution</td><td><b>Sophia Ong (Pia)</b></td><td>Ran the 2025 documentation that made this repeatable</td></tr>
